WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.770 --> 00:00:08.940
- All right thank you
so much for joining us

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:11.860
for this sixth in the series

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:14.210
of the Yale development
Dialogues Economic Policy

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:15.690
lessons from history

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:18.240
today we're going to be
discussing sustainable development

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.010
on an urbanizing planet.

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:21.490
These are virtual panel discussions

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:24.100
hosted by the Yale Economic Growth Center

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:25.840
the South Asian Studies Council

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:28.060
and the Jackson Institute
for Global Affairs.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:28.990
I'm Catherine Cheney,

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:30.610
a journalist focused
on global development,

00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:32.450
and I'll be your moderator today.

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:35.790
I'm joined by panelists
Sunil Amrith, Kate Brown,

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:37.570
Edward Glaeser, and Rory Stewart

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:39.470
whom I'll introduce in just a moment.

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:41.250
But first I wanna give a bit of context

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:44.120
on why we're talking
about this topic today.

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:46.860
So throughout history, as many of you know

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:48.390
or perhaps may study

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:50.300
population growth and urbanization

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:52.350
have been presented as a problem

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:54.610
whether it's about putting
a strain on resources

00:00:54.610 --> 00:00:57.140
or driving the climate crisis

00:00:57.140 --> 00:00:59.260
but historical and contemporary examples

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:01.870
show us how cities can
in fact, mitigate sprawl

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:05.420
reduce carbon emissions, and
in fact, foster biodiversity.

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:07.630
And we'll hear some examples of that.

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:09.320
Urbanization seems inevitable.

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:11.970
And as one of our panelists
at Glaeser puts it

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:15.450
in a conversation we had just
preparing for this session.

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:18.060
There's no pathway out of
poverty into prosperity

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:20.170
that does not run through city streets.

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:22.210
Urbanization again is happening.

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:25.490
So the question is what
role can cities play

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:28.030
in the search for green
solutions to poverty?

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:30.590
Today our panelists will
offer different perspectives

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:32.520
on what elements of organization are

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:35.560
and are not conducive to
sustainable development.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:37.360
So I'll go ahead and
introduce our panelists

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:38.520
and then we'll hear from each of them

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:40.130
and move into a conversation.

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:44.280
And I just want to remind
everyone attending this webinar

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:47.690
to please put your questions into the chat

00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:49.730
into the Q&amp;A function here in Zoom,

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:51.330
throughout the conversation.

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:54.060
And then I'll know what's
coming what's resonating

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:56.530
and work your questions in
throughout the conversation.

00:01:56.530 --> 00:01:58.040
So keep those coming.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:01:59.710
To introduce our panelists

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:01.460
first, I'd like to
introduce to Sunil Amrith.

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:04.960
He's the Renu and Anand Dhawan
Professor of History at Yale

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:07.570
and current Chair of the
South Asian Studies Council.

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:09.880
His research focuses on histories

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:11.960
and environment migration in public health

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:14.380
across South and Southeast Asia.

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:17.770
And he helped to put together this topic

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:20.690
and this panel for today,
so thank you, Sunil.

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:22.670
We'll hear more from him in a moment.

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:23.940
And Ed Glaeser is a Professor

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:25.950
of Economics at Harvard University.

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:27.700
His research focuses on cities,

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:30.540
economic growth, law and economics.

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:32.240
In particular his work has focused

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:34.750
on the determinants of city growth

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:37.820
and the role of cities as
centers of ideas, transmission.

00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:40.950
He has served as Taubman Center

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:42.100
for State and Local Government

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:45.880
and Director of the Rappaport
Institute for Greater Boston.

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:47.170
Kate Brown is a professor

00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:50.020
of Science Technology and Society at MIT.

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:52.950
Her research explores where
history science technology

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:56.210
and bio politics converged to
create large scale disasters

00:02:56.210 --> 00:02:57.890
and modernist wastelands,

00:02:57.890 --> 00:03:00.097
the author of four books most recently

00:03:00.097 --> 00:03:03.200
"Manual for Survival: A
Chernobyl Guide to the Future"

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:04.750
She's currently exploring the history

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:07.210
of what she calls plant
people, which we'll learn more

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:09.520
about who understood long before others

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:12.080
that plants communicate
have sensory capacities

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:15.840
and possess the capacity
for memory and intelligence.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:17.630
And Rory Stewart is a Senior Fellow

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:19.920
at the Yale Jackson
Institute for Global Affairs

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:22.650
where he focuses on
contemporary politics in crisis

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:25.070
and on international development
and intervention in fragile

00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:26.890
and conflict affected States.

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:28.180
Stewart, as many of you know

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:30.440
if you've tuned into these past webinars

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:32.650
previously started as
a UK Secretary of State

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:33.760
for international development

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:35.260
where he doubled the UK investment

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:37.520
in international climate and environment.

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:40.580
So I'd like to begin with
Sunil, who I mentioned is one

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:42.596
of the co-hosts of the
Yale Development Dialogues

00:03:42.596 --> 00:03:46.010
and really advocated for
this topic is one to take on.

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:48.760
So Sunil why this topic, why now

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:51.100
and why is this something that benefits

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:53.660
from bringing historical perspectives

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:55.660
together with practitioner perspectives?

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:58.060
- So if we think about

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:00.300
where we've been so far in this series

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:02.750
yet we started with a couple
of sessions on the pandemic

00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:05.820
and the implications of the
pandemic for development.

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:07.040
More recently, we've thought

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:09.350
about green pathways out of poverty.

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:11.620
And it seemed to me that this

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:13.423
the question of cities
brings those together

00:04:13.423 --> 00:04:16.380
in a very interesting way is
particularly at this moment.

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:18.270
And I do think we can benefit very much

00:04:18.270 --> 00:04:19.690
from the kind of dialogue

00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:22.600
we've had between historians
practitioners economists

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:24.800
on the question of cities

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:28.670
and sustainability in the
context of development.

00:04:28.670 --> 00:04:31.420
So about a year ago when
we were about a month

00:04:31.420 --> 00:04:32.750
or two into the pandemic

00:04:32.750 --> 00:04:36.270
I think we all saw these
photographs and I'm on social media

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:39.090
and the news of wildlife
returning to the city.

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:41.020
There were photos of Delhi

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:42.430
from where you could see the Himalayas

00:04:42.430 --> 00:04:44.443
for the first time in 20 years.

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:49.598
And on the one hand, I think
this was a wake up call

00:04:49.598 --> 00:04:53.300
for so many of us who perhaps
ignored the biodiversity

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:55.100
that has always lived in plain sight

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:57.200
in cities that we inhabit.

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:00.120
But perhaps there was also a sense

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:02.240
that this was self-indulgent that,

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:04.020
here we were talking
about wildlife returning

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:07.210
to the city when people are
suffering and losing their jobs.

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:09.256
And in a way I think it also

00:05:09.256 --> 00:05:11.520
there was a backlash against it

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:12.940
as there always has been

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:16.510
this idea that green urbanism
is in some ways an indulgence.

00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:18.390
And I wanted to think about that,

00:05:18.390 --> 00:05:20.370
the history of that in ways that

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:23.510
I'm looking forward to
hearing from Kate about that

00:05:23.510 --> 00:05:25.900
another way in which I
can see this link is,

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:27.550
we've been told many times,

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:29.240
and I think we're all convinced that

00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:30.940
one of the root causes of the pandemic

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:34.160
is the shifting wild frontier

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:36.990
the shifting frontier between
human beings and animals,

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:38.320
particularly wildlife.

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:39.790
Again, we've tended to think of that

00:05:39.790 --> 00:05:41.856
frontier as being out there,

00:05:41.856 --> 00:05:44.640
but that frontier is also right
at the heart of the cities

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:48.330
that we inhabit that frontier
is in the food systems

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:50.210
that feed us to do is that frontier

00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:51.960
is in the ways that we live

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:55.900
that frontier lives in the
implications of urban density

00:05:55.900 --> 00:05:57.630
or different kinds of urban density.

00:05:57.630 --> 00:05:59.960
So I also wanted to touch on that.

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:01.690
And finally, as a historian myself

00:06:01.690 --> 00:06:05.830
I have been struck by the
very ambivalent role of cities

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:08.530
within the entire project of development

00:06:08.530 --> 00:06:10.180
since the middle of the 20th century.

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:11.320
Now on the one hand

00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:13.210
precisely as as I put it

00:06:13.210 --> 00:06:16.210
every path to prosperity runs
through the city streets.

00:06:16.210 --> 00:06:19.450
And if you think about
development in the 1950s and '60s

00:06:19.450 --> 00:06:21.590
not just in the eyes of experts

00:06:21.590 --> 00:06:24.800
but even if you think about
Hindi cinema in the 1950s

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:26.620
the story of development is a story

00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:29.650
of leaving the countryside
and moving to the city.

00:06:29.650 --> 00:06:31.250
On the other hand, I think we've seen

00:06:31.250 --> 00:06:34.280
the second half of the 20th
century in Asia and Africa

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:36.210
a very deep anti-urbanism

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:38.870
And I think you find
this in India and China,

00:06:38.870 --> 00:06:40.560
this idea that the soul of the nation

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:41.770
lives in the countryside

00:06:41.770 --> 00:06:43.750
that the countryside is the place

00:06:43.750 --> 00:06:47.640
for sustenance and provisioning.

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:50.580
And until the 1980s you
can actually see that

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:52.520
India and China to take those two examples

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:54.400
had much lower rates of urbanization

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:56.340
than most economists predicted

00:06:56.340 --> 00:06:58.080
or that economic theory
would have predicted.

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:00.350
And then only in the late 20th century

00:07:00.350 --> 00:07:01.940
in a sense that the shackles come off.

00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:02.940
So I'd be very interested

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:05.350
in thinking about the history of ideas

00:07:05.350 --> 00:07:08.703
about the role of cities
within development as well.

00:07:10.290 --> 00:07:11.610
- Thank you and that's
something I'm looking forward

00:07:11.610 --> 00:07:13.690
to hearing from Rory on also,

00:07:13.690 --> 00:07:15.860
what are these conversations looking like

00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:18.300
in development circles

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:19.700
but I wanna bring Ed into this

00:07:19.700 --> 00:07:23.430
and Ed cities as we
have as we've discussed,

00:07:23.430 --> 00:07:26.160
are often depicted as
enemies of the environment

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:28.530
but your view is it doesn't
have to be that way.

00:07:28.530 --> 00:07:30.560
So can you expand on what it looks like

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:32.130
when it isn't that way?

00:07:32.130 --> 00:07:32.963
- Certainly thank you.

00:07:32.963 --> 00:07:35.340
And thank you for, including
me in this conversation

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:37.390
which I've been really looking forward to,

00:07:37.390 --> 00:07:39.040
I wanna jump off on Sunil's comment

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:41.530
on the thread of anti urbanisms

00:07:41.530 --> 00:07:44.590
that often shows up in
the developing world.

00:07:44.590 --> 00:07:47.210
Of course, Gandhi famously
said that the future of India

00:07:47.210 --> 00:07:49.800
is in its villages not in its cities.

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:52.650
And I cannot tell you how many times

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:57.650
I've been in a developing
world politician's office

00:07:57.800 --> 00:07:59.800
and he's asked me great
you're an expert on cities.

00:07:59.800 --> 00:08:01.610
Tell me how I stop all these poor people

00:08:01.610 --> 00:08:04.260
from coming to my city
and screwing it up, right?

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:06.643
That is frequently the
line that I've gotten.

00:08:07.864 --> 00:08:10.530
And I think that's a terrible mistake

00:08:10.530 --> 00:08:12.650
even not only obviously our cities

00:08:12.650 --> 00:08:14.380
places of economic prosperity

00:08:14.380 --> 00:08:17.990
but when you look at the urban,
rural life satisfaction gap.

00:08:17.990 --> 00:08:21.010
So the happiness gap,
that gap is nonexistent

00:08:22.336 --> 00:08:23.256
in the developed world

00:08:23.256 --> 00:08:24.610
but in the developing world, it's huge.

00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:25.470
And the country actually

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:27.360
where you have the largest
life satisfaction gap

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:29.970
in the world in our data list is India.

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:31.920
That in fact, India has the biggest gap

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:36.430
between urban happiness
and non-urban unhappiness.

00:08:36.430 --> 00:08:39.300
Now, one of the reasons why people state

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:40.500
for being anti urbanists is that

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:43.580
they see urbanism as being
antithetical to the environment.

00:08:43.580 --> 00:08:45.580
I sometimes call this the Lorax Fallacy

00:08:45.580 --> 00:08:49.120
after the Dr Seuss book,
which depicts a city building

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:51.220
running rough shot over a natural habitat.

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:53.750
And I think part of the reason

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:57.250
for that mistake is a
tendency confuse urbanization

00:08:57.250 --> 00:09:00.590
with the process of wealth accumulation

00:09:00.590 --> 00:09:03.390
the process of economic development.

00:09:03.390 --> 00:09:06.220
It is absolutely true that
as countries get richer

00:09:06.220 --> 00:09:07.480
they are gonna use more carbon

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:09.520
unless we do a lot against it, right?

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:12.500
And that's somewhat inevitable

00:09:12.500 --> 00:09:13.900
but we have to talk about how minimizing,

00:09:13.900 --> 00:09:15.630
how we can minimize that

00:09:15.630 --> 00:09:20.160
by contrast urbanization is
really about densification.

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:21.677
The economic definition of the city

00:09:21.677 --> 00:09:25.070
is the absence of physical
space between people and firms.

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:28.430
And closeness is a way of
reducing carbon emissions

00:09:28.430 --> 00:09:30.383
not a way of increasing them right?

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:33.030
Back of the envelope number that I like.

00:09:33.030 --> 00:09:34.280
And it's about 10 years old now.

00:09:34.280 --> 00:09:35.330
So take it with a grain of salt

00:09:35.330 --> 00:09:37.350
but if the great growing
economies of India

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:39.760
and China see their per
capita carbon emissions rise

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:41.750
to that scene in the
sprawling United States

00:09:41.750 --> 00:09:44.640
global carbon emissions
go up by about a tenfold.

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:48.850
If they stop at the level
seen in high wealthy

00:09:48.850 --> 00:09:50.230
but hyperdense Hong Kong

00:09:50.230 --> 00:09:52.450
global carbon emissions
go up by about 25%.

00:09:52.450 --> 00:09:54.593
It's 125% versus 25% sorry.

00:09:55.840 --> 00:09:58.500
So it's really a massive
gap in terms of this.

00:09:58.500 --> 00:09:59.700
Another way of seeing this is

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:04.070
is if you compare the
brownest city in China

00:10:04.070 --> 00:10:05.610
in terms of its household emissions

00:10:05.610 --> 00:10:07.600
as opposed to its total
industrial emissions

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:10.610
it is one fifth of the
amount of carbon per capita

00:10:10.610 --> 00:10:12.530
then the greenest city
in the United States.

00:10:12.530 --> 00:10:16.360
So there's really a huge
amount of gap is there.

00:10:16.360 --> 00:10:17.770
Now why is it the density

00:10:17.770 --> 00:10:19.790
is relatively positive
for the environment?

00:10:19.790 --> 00:10:21.270
There really are two main things

00:10:21.270 --> 00:10:23.540
that we see both in the
US and Chinese data.

00:10:23.540 --> 00:10:26.710
The first is the size of the dwelling.

00:10:26.710 --> 00:10:29.360
Typically in cities, people
are occupying smaller dwellings

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:31.580
even holding income and
family size constant.

00:10:31.580 --> 00:10:33.930
And so those smaller
dwellings just mean a lot

00:10:33.930 --> 00:10:37.550
less energy involved in
heating and cooling in China.

00:10:37.550 --> 00:10:39.330
They don't actually cool typically

00:10:39.330 --> 00:10:41.560
except for the very wealthiest
households, but they do heat.

00:10:41.560 --> 00:10:44.050
And consequently, the
cities of Northern China

00:10:44.050 --> 00:10:45.120
are much more carbon intensive

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:46.890
than the cities of Southern China.

00:10:46.890 --> 00:10:49.270
The second reason is of
course moving people around.

00:10:49.270 --> 00:10:51.510
And this is a big tension

00:10:51.510 --> 00:10:54.590
in terms of bringing
biodiversity into the city.

00:10:54.590 --> 00:10:57.610
That in fact the strongest prediction

00:10:57.610 --> 00:11:01.260
of how much carbon you
emit in your everyday life

00:11:01.260 --> 00:11:02.910
is how far the distances are

00:11:02.910 --> 00:11:05.100
between you and other people
between you and your job

00:11:05.100 --> 00:11:07.350
between you and the
places that you wanna go.

00:11:07.350 --> 00:11:08.500
The more biodiversity

00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:10.420
that you bring in
between people in cities,

00:11:10.420 --> 00:11:11.650
the more that you're expanding

00:11:11.650 --> 00:11:13.530
the distances between people

00:11:13.530 --> 00:11:14.810
and the more that you end up having people

00:11:14.810 --> 00:11:16.530
to drive longer distances

00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:18.930
and take longer commutes
in different ways.

00:11:18.930 --> 00:11:19.960
So for those reasons

00:11:19.960 --> 00:11:22.730
there's really a substantial
carbon emissions gap

00:11:22.730 --> 00:11:24.610
between people living in central New York

00:11:24.610 --> 00:11:26.530
versus people living in suburbs.

00:11:26.530 --> 00:11:28.200
So I think I'll stop there

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:29.800
but the central point being

00:11:29.800 --> 00:11:32.840
there's nothing wrong with
density for carbon emissions.

00:11:32.840 --> 00:11:36.100
In fact it's a good thing for
us to be close to one another.

00:11:36.100 --> 00:11:38.220
The challenge is as the world gets rich

00:11:38.220 --> 00:11:39.920
and I think we all want
the world to get rich

00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:41.790
how we can make sure
that the world gets rich

00:11:41.790 --> 00:11:44.090
in a way that doesn't involve
massive amounts of energy use

00:11:44.090 --> 00:11:45.540
that put the planet in peril.

00:11:46.490 --> 00:11:48.840
- Thank you, Ed and to build on that point

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:52.190
I know Kate brings a unique
insights from her research

00:11:52.190 --> 00:11:53.250
and lessons from history

00:11:53.250 --> 00:11:56.080
so Kate, I'd love your take.

00:11:56.080 --> 00:11:59.360
As Ed mentioned, closeness and

00:11:59.360 --> 00:12:01.650
and density can actually
have positive implications

00:12:01.650 --> 00:12:04.410
for the environment, but
how do we steer it that way?

00:12:04.410 --> 00:12:06.420
So what are some insights
you have to share

00:12:06.420 --> 00:12:09.210
in terms of ensuring that urbanization

00:12:09.210 --> 00:12:11.000
is not in conflict with

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:14.203
but perhaps in support
preserving biodiversity?

00:12:15.420 --> 00:12:19.074
- Yeah, well, let's look at
the past as I'm an historian.

00:12:19.074 --> 00:12:23.660
In Paris in 1900 5,000
Parisian farmers fed

00:12:23.660 --> 00:12:24.820
2 million Parisians

00:12:24.820 --> 00:12:29.820
with leftover to be shipped
to London and Washington DC

00:12:29.910 --> 00:12:34.410
where I am right now, soil
migrants migrated up to DC

00:12:34.410 --> 00:12:36.510
from the South where they lost their farms

00:12:36.510 --> 00:12:38.160
because the soil was exhausted

00:12:38.160 --> 00:12:40.320
and they could no longer produce.

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:44.810
And they started farming
there, a periphery of DC in DC

00:12:44.810 --> 00:12:48.130
but where they were allowed
to live as African-Americans

00:12:48.130 --> 00:12:51.780
and the tax records and ho housing records

00:12:51.780 --> 00:12:54.050
have an amazing fact
that among people in this

00:12:54.050 --> 00:12:58.130
and across the region of DC
rates of homeowner occupancy

00:12:58.130 --> 00:13:00.810
a significant indicator
of financial stability

00:13:00.810 --> 00:13:02.655
were some of the highest in the city

00:13:02.655 --> 00:13:07.655
59% in 1940 versus 33%
for white residents.

00:13:09.270 --> 00:13:14.270
So black residents of
Anacostia denied equal access

00:13:14.480 --> 00:13:17.820
to housing education,
public infrastructure,

00:13:17.820 --> 00:13:20.660
financing and jobs were able
to pay down on their homes.

00:13:20.660 --> 00:13:23.610
When most other white neighbors
were not able to do that.

00:13:23.610 --> 00:13:26.610
And they managed to do that
by growing their own food

00:13:26.610 --> 00:13:29.520
and trading some of it locally.

00:13:29.520 --> 00:13:33.130
Look at the Soviet Union,
1990, I was living in Moscow.

00:13:33.130 --> 00:13:35.150
Everybody talked about famine.

00:13:35.150 --> 00:13:37.210
Soviet agriculture had collapsed.

00:13:37.210 --> 00:13:41.010
Same thing happened in Cuba,
but what did people do?

00:13:41.010 --> 00:13:42.670
They got on the commuter trains.

00:13:42.670 --> 00:13:46.610
And they went out to the peri
urban areas had small plots

00:13:46.610 --> 00:13:48.200
and they grew their own potatoes

00:13:48.200 --> 00:13:52.970
by 1996 91% of all
potatoes grown in Russia

00:13:52.970 --> 00:13:55.430
were grown by small plot
gardeners, dacha gardeners

00:13:55.430 --> 00:13:59.070
and 1.5% of the arable
land in the country.

00:13:59.070 --> 00:14:01.823
So we're not talking about
that we need a lot of space.

00:14:04.110 --> 00:14:07.040
What's really sort of amazing
is that people abandoned

00:14:07.040 --> 00:14:11.650
or neglected by their government
and managed to come up

00:14:11.650 --> 00:14:13.790
with their own technological solutions.

00:14:13.790 --> 00:14:15.870
These are not primitive people

00:14:15.870 --> 00:14:18.670
that the Parisian farmers
use the mass of course,

00:14:18.670 --> 00:14:20.860
maneuver that gathered in the city.

00:14:20.860 --> 00:14:22.720
They use the urban walls

00:14:22.720 --> 00:14:24.510
but to have wall orchards

00:14:25.690 --> 00:14:28.600
in that way created
their own micro-climates

00:14:28.600 --> 00:14:30.020
and produce their own soils.

00:14:30.020 --> 00:14:32.030
And then they ended up selling those soils

00:14:32.030 --> 00:14:34.190
to farmers outside of the city.

00:14:34.190 --> 00:14:37.170
They had so many great nutrients.

00:14:37.170 --> 00:14:40.203
So what's does that matter today?

00:14:41.450 --> 00:14:44.210
I think we have one of the
greatest biodiversity ponds

00:14:44.210 --> 00:14:45.043
we have right now

00:14:45.043 --> 00:14:49.343
is a biodiversity of the
human gut microbiome.

00:14:50.250 --> 00:14:52.570
The people in industrial North have this

00:14:52.570 --> 00:14:55.780
really streamlined
biodiversity in their gut

00:14:55.780 --> 00:14:58.650
and that has all kinds
of health repercussions

00:14:58.650 --> 00:15:02.380
and starts in childhood
and continues into old age.

00:15:02.380 --> 00:15:06.367
So how do we what we've
basically done in cities

00:15:06.367 --> 00:15:08.540
and suburbs is

00:15:08.540 --> 00:15:10.900
and in the conventional
industrial farming areas

00:15:10.900 --> 00:15:12.890
is engineered ourselves

00:15:12.890 --> 00:15:15.140
to the very margins of our environments.

00:15:15.140 --> 00:15:18.290
And so gardening puts
people microbially back

00:15:18.290 --> 00:15:19.540
into their environments,

00:15:19.540 --> 00:15:22.930
and you can do it in very tiny
spaces where you have these

00:15:22.930 --> 00:15:27.010
or we've got records of people
producing masses of food

00:15:27.010 --> 00:15:30.213
six crops a year on a
10th of an acre of land.

00:15:31.290 --> 00:15:33.360
So I appreciate what Ed is talking about

00:15:33.360 --> 00:15:37.760
in terms of density and I don't
think it has to annihilate

00:15:37.760 --> 00:15:41.420
the prospect of biodiversity.

00:15:41.420 --> 00:15:43.570
In fact, if you look at American cities

00:15:43.570 --> 00:15:47.790
most of our a good portion, let me say 30.

00:15:47.790 --> 00:15:49.620
Sometimes 50% of our real estate

00:15:49.620 --> 00:15:53.310
is given as devoted to cars,
moving them and parking them.

00:15:53.310 --> 00:15:56.850
And most of those cars sit
around 90% of the time.

00:15:56.850 --> 00:15:59.510
Those cities of the future
can't have cars anymore.

00:15:59.510 --> 00:16:02.330
I think that the Netherlands
have just decided to ban cars

00:16:02.330 --> 00:16:03.900
in their major cities.

00:16:03.900 --> 00:16:06.600
So what if this doesn't
take an act of God.

00:16:06.600 --> 00:16:08.470
It doesn't take an,
even an act of Congress,

00:16:08.470 --> 00:16:11.830
but just a municipal
ordinance shift devote

00:16:13.313 --> 00:16:15.130
that territory that's been
given over to parking,

00:16:15.130 --> 00:16:16.303
devoted to plants.

00:16:17.590 --> 00:16:20.190
Every new apartment has
to have a gardening plot

00:16:20.190 --> 00:16:22.210
rather than a parking lot.

00:16:22.210 --> 00:16:24.210
I think we could have a whole new world.

00:16:25.750 --> 00:16:26.583
I agree with you.

00:16:26.583 --> 00:16:29.510
And I'm curious, we
hopefully, in our discussion

00:16:29.510 --> 00:16:31.890
we can talk about while that is certainly

00:16:31.890 --> 00:16:35.330
an appealing vision of what
our future could look like.

00:16:35.330 --> 00:16:37.420
What are the real challenges
that stand in the way

00:16:37.420 --> 00:16:38.930
in terms of implementing that vision

00:16:38.930 --> 00:16:40.880
which actually brings me into Rory

00:16:40.880 --> 00:16:45.640
and Rory we've heard that urbanization

00:16:45.640 --> 00:16:47.640
and sustainable development biodiversity

00:16:47.640 --> 00:16:50.470
do not have to be in
conflict with one another.

00:16:50.470 --> 00:16:52.480
In fact, they can support one another.

00:16:52.480 --> 00:16:54.870
And yet, as Sunil mentioned

00:16:54.870 --> 00:16:57.750
cities often play an ambivalent role in

00:16:57.750 --> 00:16:58.870
in the project of development.

00:16:58.870 --> 00:17:00.270
And in fact, if anything,

00:17:00.270 --> 00:17:04.070
maybe there's an anti
organism lens that many take.

00:17:04.070 --> 00:17:08.840
So what was your experience
working within DFID

00:17:08.840 --> 00:17:11.080
now the STDO in the UK in terms

00:17:11.080 --> 00:17:16.080
of what this looked like in
an international aid agenda?

00:17:16.190 --> 00:17:19.330
- So let me first preface by saying

00:17:19.330 --> 00:17:22.630
I'm gonna start with a
focus on the UK aid agency

00:17:22.630 --> 00:17:24.150
but it's worth looking at DFID

00:17:24.150 --> 00:17:26.160
because it was one of the most

00:17:26.160 --> 00:17:27.407
thoughtful progressive agencies.

00:17:27.407 --> 00:17:30.670
And to some extent, the way
that it looked at the world

00:17:30.670 --> 00:17:31.990
is characteristic of the way

00:17:31.990 --> 00:17:32.823
that many people in the World Bank

00:17:32.823 --> 00:17:35.827
and other financial agencies
looked at the world.

00:17:37.080 --> 00:17:39.160
The first thing to understand is that

00:17:39.160 --> 00:17:43.007
at the core of the work of a DFID

00:17:43.007 --> 00:17:44.810
and many of these bilateral agencies

00:17:44.810 --> 00:17:46.110
is the idea of focusing

00:17:46.110 --> 00:17:48.520
on the very poorest people in the world

00:17:48.520 --> 00:17:50.960
in particular, the
better focusing on people

00:17:50.960 --> 00:17:52.610
who are living on under $2 a day.

00:17:53.500 --> 00:17:57.570
And that means that so
much of the programming

00:17:57.570 --> 00:18:00.580
is about trying to reach those individuals

00:18:01.420 --> 00:18:02.520
that has meant for example,

00:18:02.520 --> 00:18:04.190
the real passion for cash transfers

00:18:04.190 --> 00:18:07.210
an idea of giving cash to families

00:18:07.210 --> 00:18:08.640
in order to transform their lives,

00:18:08.640 --> 00:18:10.190
either in a monthly payment

00:18:10.190 --> 00:18:13.520
or a one-time payment to help
them set up a micro business

00:18:14.890 --> 00:18:17.600
following on from that
will be large programs,

00:18:17.600 --> 00:18:19.500
particularly around primary education

00:18:19.500 --> 00:18:20.743
and primary health care.

00:18:23.130 --> 00:18:25.950
And a lot of the energy of the agencies

00:18:25.950 --> 00:18:27.850
is absorbed in that kind of work.

00:18:28.730 --> 00:18:31.810
What isn't done by these
agencies traditionally

00:18:32.710 --> 00:18:36.090
are very expensive large
infrastructure projects

00:18:36.090 --> 00:18:38.458
that's become less and
less fashionable, right?

00:18:38.458 --> 00:18:40.220
There are other ways of funding that

00:18:40.220 --> 00:18:41.450
some of that from the private sector

00:18:41.450 --> 00:18:43.950
some of that from development banks,

00:18:43.950 --> 00:18:46.640
but by and large the idea has been

00:18:46.640 --> 00:18:51.640
to take the emphasis off, dams, roads

00:18:51.720 --> 00:18:54.960
and onto the question of how
you work with very poor people.

00:18:54.960 --> 00:18:58.070
Now, fast forward, what
is the problem then

00:18:58.070 --> 00:19:00.830
that we confront the problem effectively

00:19:00.830 --> 00:19:04.580
is that that model of
working with very poor people

00:19:06.160 --> 00:19:08.020
basically in people's minds

00:19:08.020 --> 00:19:11.854
and someone like Tanzania
has a rural feeling right?

00:19:11.854 --> 00:19:13.670
The general impression is that

00:19:13.670 --> 00:19:14.830
you're turning up in a remote

00:19:14.830 --> 00:19:18.330
rural area where people have
very relaxes to services.

00:19:18.330 --> 00:19:20.050
And you're worrying about how to set up

00:19:20.050 --> 00:19:22.760
a primary health clinic
or a primary school

00:19:22.760 --> 00:19:24.310
and how to work particularly on,

00:19:24.310 --> 00:19:26.970
for example agricultural
extension development work,

00:19:26.970 --> 00:19:29.540
making sure that people
in those communities can

00:19:29.540 --> 00:19:32.470
get more crops out of
a given amount of land

00:19:32.470 --> 00:19:34.380
and maybe get some loans to enable them

00:19:34.380 --> 00:19:37.270
to access fertilizers,
pesticides, et cetera.

00:19:37.270 --> 00:19:40.360
But in fact, the reality of
increasingly what's happening

00:19:40.360 --> 00:19:43.660
in some of that Tanzania is
people are moving to cities.

00:19:43.660 --> 00:19:45.050
So you turn up in a city

00:19:45.050 --> 00:19:49.230
and you find that 85% youth unemployment

00:19:49.230 --> 00:19:51.150
you find it an incredible number of people

00:19:51.150 --> 00:19:53.630
in their late teens and
early 20s who swarmed in

00:19:53.630 --> 00:19:55.620
as sort made swarmed as a bad word

00:19:55.620 --> 00:19:57.350
but have common and very large number

00:19:57.350 --> 00:19:58.980
it's from rural areas

00:19:58.980 --> 00:20:01.493
and are struggling to
find any form of job.

00:20:02.630 --> 00:20:05.510
And the city is expanding
unbelievably quickly.

00:20:05.510 --> 00:20:08.480
I mean, one of the challenges
in the developing world

00:20:08.480 --> 00:20:13.480
is that when I was
first in Kabul, in 2001,

00:20:16.580 --> 00:20:18.470
the total population of the city

00:20:18.470 --> 00:20:21.470
was approximately 400,000 people.

00:20:21.470 --> 00:20:25.360
Today the population of
Kabul will be up pushing

00:20:25.360 --> 00:20:29.183
probably four or 5 million, and
that's happened in 20 years.

00:20:30.100 --> 00:20:32.990
And when that happens,
everything changes right?

00:20:32.990 --> 00:20:36.150
The water vanishes, because
you start tapping a water table

00:20:36.150 --> 00:20:37.980
which simply doesn't have
enough water to keep the city.

00:20:37.980 --> 00:20:39.610
That sounds great.

00:20:39.610 --> 00:20:41.430
There is no sewage infrastructure.

00:20:41.430 --> 00:20:43.630
There's no proper pipe,
water infrastructure

00:20:45.190 --> 00:20:48.250
informal settlements
springing up on every hillside

00:20:48.250 --> 00:20:50.423
and often collapsing and landslides.

00:20:51.850 --> 00:20:53.890
There is no provision of public space.

00:20:53.890 --> 00:20:56.100
There's no green space that's made parks.

00:20:56.100 --> 00:20:58.800
Nobody's really thinking
about mass transit

00:20:58.800 --> 00:21:02.120
subsidy no underground system
getting into this neighbor.

00:21:02.120 --> 00:21:04.700
So what does the development agency do?

00:21:04.700 --> 00:21:06.120
And they're the problem
and I'll finish on this

00:21:06.120 --> 00:21:07.790
they are the problem comes,

00:21:07.790 --> 00:21:10.420
which is the mindset of
the development agency

00:21:10.420 --> 00:21:11.690
is still very much focused

00:21:11.690 --> 00:21:14.012
on the very poorest people in villages.

00:21:14.012 --> 00:21:17.260
And thinking about how you
can change someone's life

00:21:17.260 --> 00:21:19.633
with let's say 40, $50 a year.

00:21:20.700 --> 00:21:24.690
Suddenly you are in the
environment of Kabul

00:21:24.690 --> 00:21:26.240
or Dar es Salaam

00:21:26.240 --> 00:21:28.010
Well, what is actually needed

00:21:28.010 --> 00:21:31.200
is literally billions of
dollars worth of investment

00:21:31.200 --> 00:21:35.140
in laying the basic infrastructure
for functioning city

00:21:35.140 --> 00:21:37.480
and making some very,
very difficult decisions

00:21:37.480 --> 00:21:39.120
challenging property developers

00:21:39.120 --> 00:21:44.120
who are building very dangerous,
unsafe, unsound buildings,

00:21:44.160 --> 00:21:47.470
thinking about how you could
generate a sustainable urbanism

00:21:47.470 --> 00:21:49.150
thinking about how you might protect

00:21:49.150 --> 00:21:51.230
some of the historical
heritage of the city

00:21:51.230 --> 00:21:53.160
thinking about how you
create your green spaces

00:21:53.160 --> 00:21:55.293
how you get people walking, right?

00:21:56.640 --> 00:21:59.080
And then that is an enormous problem

00:21:59.080 --> 00:22:00.610
and enormous problem in mobilizing

00:22:00.610 --> 00:22:01.920
sufficient resources to do this

00:22:01.920 --> 00:22:04.470
because this stuff is
incredibly expensive.

00:22:04.470 --> 00:22:06.700
I mean, creating an underground system

00:22:06.700 --> 00:22:10.170
is an astonishingly expensive
thing to do in any city.

00:22:10.170 --> 00:22:12.390
And very difficult for
people psychologically

00:22:12.390 --> 00:22:14.790
to justify you're in a very poor country.

00:22:14.790 --> 00:22:16.630
What are you doing spending

00:22:16.630 --> 00:22:18.970
literally billions of dollars, right?

00:22:18.970 --> 00:22:20.290
To set up one of these systems.

00:22:20.290 --> 00:22:21.400
And yet you have to do it.

00:22:21.400 --> 00:22:23.740
If you don't make that investment now,

00:22:23.740 --> 00:22:27.250
credibly difficult to retrofit.

00:22:27.250 --> 00:22:28.860
I mean, almost impossible now in Kabul

00:22:28.860 --> 00:22:30.950
to work out how you actually
put that infrastructure

00:22:30.950 --> 00:22:33.450
in because so much rapid development

00:22:33.450 --> 00:22:34.670
has happened in the last 20 years,

00:22:34.670 --> 00:22:35.503
that it's very difficult to work out

00:22:35.503 --> 00:22:36.693
where the spaces to get the sewage

00:22:36.693 --> 00:22:38.330
and the water turning left lane

00:22:38.330 --> 00:22:40.210
and underground system

00:22:40.210 --> 00:22:41.300
(mumbles)

00:22:41.300 --> 00:22:42.728
Okay, I'm gonna finish on that

00:22:42.728 --> 00:22:43.561
because I want this to be more

00:22:43.561 --> 00:22:45.209
of a conversation in my lecture.

00:22:45.209 --> 00:22:46.230
- Yeah.
- But,

00:22:46.230 --> 00:22:47.760
somewhere in the problem is

00:22:48.740 --> 00:22:50.310
different we'll talk about urbanism

00:22:50.310 --> 00:22:52.160
but it will find it very very difficult

00:22:52.160 --> 00:22:54.270
to make the types of investments necessary

00:22:54.270 --> 00:22:56.543
to really help our slum
carbon in the future.

00:22:57.450 --> 00:22:58.283
- Thank you, Rory.

00:22:58.283 --> 00:22:59.890
Well, that's exactly
where I'd love to begin.

00:22:59.890 --> 00:23:01.770
I just love to bring any of our panelists

00:23:01.770 --> 00:23:04.200
and hearing this challenge from Rory

00:23:04.200 --> 00:23:07.230
that the mindset of development agencies

00:23:07.230 --> 00:23:11.030
is focused on the very
poorest living in villages,

00:23:11.030 --> 00:23:14.770
not some of the challenges in cities,

00:23:14.770 --> 00:23:16.470
any thoughts from any of you

00:23:16.470 --> 00:23:20.070
just reactions to why that is
or whether that could change.

00:23:20.070 --> 00:23:21.920
And if it were to change

00:23:21.920 --> 00:23:25.770
where lessons from
history might be valuable

00:23:25.770 --> 00:23:27.160
- I don't think that's a mistake at all.

00:23:27.160 --> 00:23:29.810
So in terms of disclosure,
I need to need to say

00:23:29.810 --> 00:23:30.750
that since we're talking about different

00:23:30.750 --> 00:23:33.260
that I co lead the
international growth centers

00:23:33.260 --> 00:23:35.180
cities program, which is different funded.

00:23:35.180 --> 00:23:39.367
So we certainly do have a deep
gratitude to two different.

00:23:39.367 --> 00:23:42.100
And I think that in terms
of the flow of funds

00:23:42.100 --> 00:23:43.700
at least given current development budgets

00:23:43.700 --> 00:23:44.860
that's exactly right.

00:23:44.860 --> 00:23:46.330
On other hand, it is also appropriate

00:23:46.330 --> 00:23:49.270
for entities like the IGC to do research

00:23:49.270 --> 00:23:51.820
as I do on infrastructure
in the developing world

00:23:51.820 --> 00:23:54.080
in particular infrastructure
in developing world cities

00:23:54.080 --> 00:23:56.850
and to try and help those
countries figure out

00:23:56.850 --> 00:23:59.120
how to make those systems functional.

00:23:59.120 --> 00:24:01.730
I mean, for example, you
know, Rory has pointed out

00:24:01.730 --> 00:24:05.250
the unbelievable price of
underground subway systems.

00:24:05.250 --> 00:24:06.920
There's an old joke that 50 years

00:24:06.920 --> 00:24:08.550
of transportation economics at Harvard

00:24:08.550 --> 00:24:12.545
can be boiled down to four
words, bus, good, train bad.

00:24:12.545 --> 00:24:14.250
And in fact, bus rapid transit

00:24:14.250 --> 00:24:16.636
particularly the developing
world is a vastly

00:24:16.636 --> 00:24:19.490
more cost-effective
means of working things.

00:24:19.490 --> 00:24:22.890
That being said, I think
in a post COVID world

00:24:22.890 --> 00:24:27.290
there is a view that at
least it's a view that I have

00:24:27.290 --> 00:24:29.370
that in fact, COVID
should be a wake up call

00:24:29.370 --> 00:24:31.930
that the wealthy world should actually

00:24:31.930 --> 00:24:33.400
especially the United States

00:24:33.400 --> 00:24:35.220
should massively increase its commitment

00:24:35.220 --> 00:24:36.230
to developing world budgets.

00:24:36.230 --> 00:24:38.900
And that investment should be motivated

00:24:38.900 --> 00:24:42.200
by primarily by concerns
for global public health.

00:24:42.200 --> 00:24:44.450
And I don't think spending on roads

00:24:44.450 --> 00:24:45.640
is obviously part of that

00:24:45.640 --> 00:24:48.510
but spending on sewers might very well be

00:24:48.510 --> 00:24:50.380
if you have the view that

00:24:50.380 --> 00:24:52.790
currently having unhealthy water systems

00:24:52.790 --> 00:24:54.610
leads to the proliferation of superbugs

00:24:54.610 --> 00:24:56.320
that are antibiotic resistant.

00:24:56.320 --> 00:24:58.970
And it is amazing that
the work of my colleague,

00:24:58.970 --> 00:25:02.050
Dr. Marcella Alsan at the Kennedy School

00:25:02.050 --> 00:25:03.600
finds that in Hyderabad

00:25:03.600 --> 00:25:05.750
the number of women who enter the hospital

00:25:05.750 --> 00:25:06.980
simply because they were pregnant

00:25:06.980 --> 00:25:09.160
but were then tested for
antibiotic resistant diseases.

00:25:09.160 --> 00:25:10.980
The numbers of them who
had them in their guts

00:25:10.980 --> 00:25:12.180
was just enormous, right?

00:25:12.180 --> 00:25:13.870
So we are really sitting
on a time bomb here

00:25:13.870 --> 00:25:16.853
and investing in water could
be a way of fixing that.

00:25:18.174 --> 00:25:19.970
So I think those forms of
investment makes sense.

00:25:19.970 --> 00:25:21.630
And I think it is possible

00:25:21.630 --> 00:25:22.770
that you might even want things

00:25:22.770 --> 00:25:24.640
that are more of a quid pro quo,

00:25:24.640 --> 00:25:26.310
which is you get the money in exchange

00:25:26.310 --> 00:25:28.730
for a serious monitoring devices

00:25:28.730 --> 00:25:30.580
that actually look out
for outbreaks of disease

00:25:30.580 --> 00:25:33.700
or for the same type of hygienic policies

00:25:33.700 --> 00:25:35.010
that we had in the 19th century

00:25:35.010 --> 00:25:37.280
that kept animals away from humans, right?

00:25:37.280 --> 00:25:39.400
So part of the critical
thing is we wanna sort of

00:25:39.400 --> 00:25:41.210
separate out nature and humanity

00:25:41.210 --> 00:25:44.300
so that we don't have this
sort of cross species,

00:25:44.300 --> 00:25:46.670
zoonotic diseases striking as we've

00:25:46.670 --> 00:25:48.590
currently the whole world
has been suffering from

