00:36:17.480 --> 00:36:18.980
Kate, you just brought
up a good point that

00:36:18.980 --> 00:36:21.810
when you kind of shift your mindset

00:36:21.810 --> 00:36:23.180
it unlocks new solutions.

00:36:23.180 --> 00:36:25.280
and if we don't have these
kinds of conversations

00:36:25.280 --> 00:36:27.360
we don't even see those
potential solutions.

00:36:27.360 --> 00:36:28.520
So go ahead, Sunil.

00:36:28.520 --> 00:36:31.110
- I just want to pick up
on what Rory was saying

00:36:31.110 --> 00:36:32.330
and Kate also touched on

00:36:32.330 --> 00:36:35.800
in terms of the kind of
disconnected thinking,

00:36:35.800 --> 00:36:37.170
and to turn that back to your question,

00:36:37.170 --> 00:36:38.670
Catherine, about obstacles

00:36:38.670 --> 00:36:42.630
and I think we need to
acknowledge sometimes

00:36:42.630 --> 00:36:45.350
that the obstacles are very serious ones,

00:36:45.350 --> 00:36:48.090
the lawns most recent report estimates

00:36:48.090 --> 00:36:50.800
that 1.6 million people in India diet

00:36:50.800 --> 00:36:52.550
because of air pollution last year.

00:36:53.420 --> 00:36:55.410
And it seems to me that the
specific problem of pollution

00:36:55.410 --> 00:36:58.350
in country like India, just
take that as an example,

00:36:58.350 --> 00:37:00.600
is almost sort of worst of both worlds

00:37:00.600 --> 00:37:03.690
because you have that as a combined

00:37:03.690 --> 00:37:06.770
and conjoined product of rural poverty,

00:37:06.770 --> 00:37:08.230
including energy, poverty.

00:37:08.230 --> 00:37:11.560
I mean a lot of this is black carbon

00:37:11.560 --> 00:37:14.650
being burned domestically.

00:37:14.650 --> 00:37:16.240
It's about crop burning

00:37:16.240 --> 00:37:18.170
but it's also about completely

00:37:18.170 --> 00:37:21.250
unrestrained automobile
use in India cities.

00:37:21.250 --> 00:37:26.250
And I wonder in which what
kind of problem does pollution

00:37:27.620 --> 00:37:29.810
pose for development?

00:37:29.810 --> 00:37:31.990
I mean, I think that there
are all sorts of ways in which

00:37:31.990 --> 00:37:32.960
and if we think about public health,

00:37:32.960 --> 00:37:34.157
we're thinking about cities,

00:37:34.157 --> 00:37:35.970
we need to be thinking
about the air as well.

00:37:35.970 --> 00:37:39.260
And it just seems to me
a quintessential example

00:37:39.260 --> 00:37:42.370
of how multiple agendas

00:37:42.370 --> 00:37:44.430
might be pushing in very
different directions,

00:37:44.430 --> 00:37:46.100
because on the one hand

00:37:46.100 --> 00:37:48.720
one of the arguments
is that the reason why

00:37:48.720 --> 00:37:51.540
a crop burning has increased
significantly in India

00:37:51.540 --> 00:37:52.680
over the last 20 years or so

00:37:52.680 --> 00:37:54.310
is because of the green revolution.

00:37:54.310 --> 00:37:56.780
It's because of the third annual crop.

00:37:56.780 --> 00:37:59.320
It's because of the ways in which,

00:37:59.320 --> 00:38:02.010
the nature of the agricultural system

00:38:02.010 --> 00:38:04.210
has been pushing in that way.

00:38:04.210 --> 00:38:06.580
On the other hand, there's
this sort of urban rural divide

00:38:06.580 --> 00:38:07.527
where you have the politicians

00:38:07.527 --> 00:38:09.740
and Delhi blaming Haryana and Punjab

00:38:09.740 --> 00:38:11.010
for the pollution in Delhi

00:38:11.010 --> 00:38:14.210
which is only really
one part of the problem.

00:38:14.210 --> 00:38:16.390
So again this is just a call

00:38:16.390 --> 00:38:18.610
from all of these kinds of conversations,

00:38:18.610 --> 00:38:20.650
which point out some of the contradictions

00:38:20.650 --> 00:38:22.170
that I think that sort of

00:38:22.170 --> 00:38:26.040
often leading these negative outcomes,

00:38:26.040 --> 00:38:27.890
rather than the kinds of synergies

00:38:27.890 --> 00:38:30.750
that both Kate had in their
different ways for talking about

00:38:30.750 --> 00:38:33.530
- Some, not amazingly the
anti-poverty program and Riga

00:38:33.530 --> 00:38:35.630
Actually appears to be somewhat
to blame for air pollution.

00:38:35.630 --> 00:38:38.270
So this is the work of Patrick
Bayer at the world bank

00:38:38.270 --> 00:38:39.970
that is very well-meaning program

00:38:39.970 --> 00:38:42.410
to raise wages for ordinary Indians,

00:38:42.410 --> 00:38:44.980
by raising the cost of
labor intensive agriculture

00:38:44.980 --> 00:38:47.200
shifted to move to capital
intensive agriculture.

00:38:47.200 --> 00:38:49.150
That was actually worse
for the environment.

00:38:49.150 --> 00:38:51.000
I just wanted to follow
up on Rory's comment.

00:38:51.000 --> 00:38:52.480
And clearly I'm sort of the most..

00:38:52.480 --> 00:38:53.313
I seem to be the most

00:38:53.313 --> 00:38:55.600
pro infrastructure person
on this on this panel,

00:38:55.600 --> 00:38:58.640
but even I am not particularly
in favor of DFID ,

00:38:58.640 --> 00:39:00.360
all of a sudden getting
into the world bank

00:39:00.360 --> 00:39:02.540
circa 1955 kinds of businesses.

00:39:02.540 --> 00:39:04.550
The thing that DFID can bring

00:39:04.550 --> 00:39:06.920
is the part of infrastructure
that it actually knows

00:39:06.920 --> 00:39:08.600
which is the governance part.

00:39:08.600 --> 00:39:10.640
I mean, infrastructure is
not just about structure.

00:39:10.640 --> 00:39:12.210
It's about what are the institutions

00:39:12.210 --> 00:39:13.870
that you're using to
manage that infrastructure

00:39:13.870 --> 00:39:16.030
and what are the incentives
you're putting into place

00:39:16.030 --> 00:39:17.890
to get people to connect
to that infrastructure.

00:39:17.890 --> 00:39:20.160
And that's a ubiquitous
problem in the developing world

00:39:20.160 --> 00:39:21.230
that you'll build a water main

00:39:21.230 --> 00:39:22.720
and you'll have a white elephant project

00:39:22.720 --> 00:39:23.930
that people aren't connected to.

00:39:23.930 --> 00:39:25.440
This was exactly the same problem

00:39:25.440 --> 00:39:28.140
that New York faced in the 1840s, 1850s,

00:39:28.140 --> 00:39:29.430
when it built the Croton Aqueduct

00:39:29.430 --> 00:39:32.160
and 1000s and 1000s of poor
New Yorkers didn't connect

00:39:32.160 --> 00:39:33.640
and continue to die of cholera.

00:39:33.640 --> 00:39:34.473
In fact, my great, great

00:39:34.473 --> 00:39:36.117
great grandfather died of cholera in 1849.

00:39:36.117 --> 00:39:37.450
And in New York seven years

00:39:37.450 --> 00:39:39.610
after the Croton Aqueduct open, right?

00:39:39.610 --> 00:39:41.450
You needed to have a
policy new York's case

00:39:41.450 --> 00:39:43.250
which actually penalized tenement owners

00:39:43.250 --> 00:39:44.810
who didn't connect to the water system

00:39:44.810 --> 00:39:46.560
to actually close that last mile problem.

00:39:46.560 --> 00:39:48.980
In some cases might want subsidies instead

00:39:48.980 --> 00:39:52.170
but in any case, that
is the business of DFID

00:39:52.170 --> 00:39:53.470
it is to think about things like that

00:39:53.470 --> 00:39:55.500
and to figure out what
our reasonable lessons

00:39:55.500 --> 00:39:56.830
that can be drawn from the history,

00:39:56.830 --> 00:40:00.190
both in the developing world
and the developed world.

00:40:00.190 --> 00:40:01.869
- Thank you, Ed, so we've heard these--

00:40:01.869 --> 00:40:02.702
(mumbles)

00:40:02.702 --> 00:40:03.993
Go a head Rory.

00:40:03.993 --> 00:40:06.350
- I mean, on that, I think
we'll say there is a question

00:40:06.350 --> 00:40:07.650
around community mobilization.

00:40:07.650 --> 00:40:10.937
I mean, an enormous amount
of what works in Kabul

00:40:10.937 --> 00:40:13.030
and actually getting people
to connect to water supply

00:40:13.030 --> 00:40:15.970
or connect to electricity
or connect to sewerage

00:40:15.970 --> 00:40:18.120
in volts and very patient conversations

00:40:18.120 --> 00:40:19.550
almost household by household

00:40:19.550 --> 00:40:21.730
and really trying to understand

00:40:21.730 --> 00:40:23.430
why it is people didn't want to connect

00:40:23.430 --> 00:40:24.277
what they were worried about

00:40:24.277 --> 00:40:25.110
but they worried about

00:40:25.110 --> 00:40:28.040
the fact they'd be caught
in some taxation net,

00:40:28.040 --> 00:40:30.160
was that the cost of the connection

00:40:30.160 --> 00:40:31.840
that was putting them off

00:40:31.840 --> 00:40:35.330
was it some dispute the
neighbor, if a landownership

00:40:35.330 --> 00:40:36.980
through which the pipe was moving

00:40:37.820 --> 00:40:40.440
that too is something that

00:40:40.440 --> 00:40:43.010
is sort of counter-intuitive in a way that

00:40:44.060 --> 00:40:46.550
if infrastructure is
often led by engineers

00:40:46.550 --> 00:40:48.820
they're often people who don't necessarily

00:40:48.820 --> 00:40:50.950
have an intuitive feeling for how you work

00:40:50.950 --> 00:40:53.810
house to house in a poor area.

00:40:53.810 --> 00:40:58.480
I mean I remember working with
a sewage engineer in Kabul

00:40:58.480 --> 00:41:00.610
who kept saying it's splendid

00:41:00.610 --> 00:41:02.390
kind of jump and sewage engineer

00:41:02.390 --> 00:41:04.040
who kept saying in relation
to almost everything,

00:41:04.040 --> 00:41:05.590
but this doesn't make sense.

00:41:05.590 --> 00:41:07.540
Why wouldn't they do this right?

00:41:07.540 --> 00:41:12.320
And sometimes self skills to
get around this question of

00:41:12.320 --> 00:41:14.500
but this doesn't make sense
why isn't this happening

00:41:14.500 --> 00:41:16.890
in the logical way that
I expect essentially.

00:41:16.890 --> 00:41:18.210
- Yeah great point.

00:41:18.210 --> 00:41:21.060
I wanted to get your take on

00:41:21.060 --> 00:41:23.210
kind of circling back to
what we discussed earlier,

00:41:23.210 --> 00:41:24.840
why this topic, why now?

00:41:24.840 --> 00:41:28.030
So Sunil mentioned air pollution

00:41:28.030 --> 00:41:33.030
and the health implications
of air pollution

00:41:33.510 --> 00:41:34.710
being increasingly clear.

00:41:34.710 --> 00:41:38.290
We also have, COVID-19
serving as a wake-up call

00:41:38.290 --> 00:41:40.700
and these interactions between human

00:41:40.700 --> 00:41:43.693
and animals happening in
ways they haven't before.

00:41:44.690 --> 00:41:47.380
So we've talked about some
of the challenges, right?

00:41:47.380 --> 00:41:49.410
These conversations not happening,

00:41:49.410 --> 00:41:52.300
or maybe some of these organizations

00:41:52.300 --> 00:41:55.010
not leaning into their skill sets

00:41:55.010 --> 00:41:57.870
for how they could best
address this problem

00:41:57.870 --> 00:41:59.583
or seize this opportunity.

00:42:01.970 --> 00:42:04.250
What's your sense of whether,

00:42:04.250 --> 00:42:06.330
beyond this conversation we're having

00:42:06.330 --> 00:42:07.610
or conversations that seem

00:42:07.610 --> 00:42:09.730
to happen kind of on the sidelines, right?

00:42:09.730 --> 00:42:14.687
Not in the mainstream in terms
of ensuring that urbanization

00:42:14.687 --> 00:42:17.590
and sustainable development are actually

00:42:17.590 --> 00:42:19.700
working in cooperation with one another

00:42:19.700 --> 00:42:21.320
is this an opportunity for real change?

00:42:21.320 --> 00:42:24.470
Do you get a sense that read,
any kind of turning point

00:42:24.470 --> 00:42:28.000
there's any momentum here
that there's an opportunity

00:42:28.000 --> 00:42:29.580
or as part of the problem that

00:42:29.580 --> 00:42:31.680
this is going to remain on the sidelines,

00:42:31.680 --> 00:42:32.793
not in the mainstream?

00:42:34.560 --> 00:42:37.610
- Well, one of the most
Google phrases in 2020

00:42:37.610 --> 00:42:40.193
in Russian was how do you plant potatoes?

00:42:41.820 --> 00:42:44.560
And I think there has been this return to

00:42:44.560 --> 00:42:45.710
people are stuck at home.

00:42:45.710 --> 00:42:48.980
People are returning to
their immediate environments

00:42:48.980 --> 00:42:51.040
and starting to sort of delve in

00:42:51.040 --> 00:42:55.030
I think we're where you now
know that we can make changes

00:42:55.030 --> 00:42:58.190
at a lightning speed on a global scale

00:42:58.190 --> 00:43:00.580
in ways that we hadn't
thought were possible before

00:43:00.580 --> 00:43:03.960
when it comes to a global
public health situation.

00:43:03.960 --> 00:43:05.270
As Sunil pointed out,

00:43:05.270 --> 00:43:09.560
we have global public
health crises all the time

00:43:09.560 --> 00:43:12.620
that have to do with poisoned environments

00:43:12.620 --> 00:43:15.630
but those are slow public health problems.

00:43:15.630 --> 00:43:17.120
We really only seem to be good

00:43:17.120 --> 00:43:20.100
when it's infectious
diseases is immediate.

00:43:20.100 --> 00:43:22.270
It strikes person, you know, one day.

00:43:22.270 --> 00:43:25.250
And then the next day there
a major difference in health.

00:43:25.250 --> 00:43:28.320
It's the slow sicknesses

00:43:28.320 --> 00:43:32.480
that have to do with contaminants,
congealing, and lungs

00:43:33.350 --> 00:43:37.520
contaminants going into flesh
and slowly creating cancers

00:43:37.520 --> 00:43:40.240
and tumors auto-immune disorders.

00:43:40.240 --> 00:43:41.110
Those are the problems that

00:43:41.110 --> 00:43:43.197
we've been really bad about dealing with.

00:43:43.197 --> 00:43:45.410
And that I would hope that the pandemic

00:43:45.410 --> 00:43:47.720
would get us to focus on.

00:43:47.720 --> 00:43:50.810
And then I think we would
maybe reorient our priorities

00:43:50.810 --> 00:43:53.240
less to the emergency situation

00:43:53.240 --> 00:43:56.210
and more to the long slow violence

00:43:56.210 --> 00:43:58.183
of environmental problems.

00:43:59.850 --> 00:44:02.260
- Great framing of why now is different

00:44:03.110 --> 00:44:03.943
and what we've learned in this moment.

00:44:03.943 --> 00:44:05.587
Did others wanna jump in on that?

00:44:07.130 --> 00:44:08.270
I think Rory maybe--

00:44:08.270 --> 00:44:10.740
- One thing I suppose that strikes me

00:44:11.748 --> 00:44:14.530
is in thinking this
through, it's very important

00:44:14.530 --> 00:44:17.170
to try to put yourself in the mindset

00:44:17.170 --> 00:44:19.840
of somebody working in the municipality

00:44:19.840 --> 00:44:21.440
of the developing country

00:44:21.440 --> 00:44:24.420
and really try to think
through what's going on.

00:44:24.420 --> 00:44:28.130
So that could include, for example

00:44:28.130 --> 00:44:31.350
at an extreme level corruption, right?

00:44:31.350 --> 00:44:33.810
Many, many people in municipality
are extremely poorly paid.

00:44:33.810 --> 00:44:36.120
And many of the decisions
that they're making

00:44:36.120 --> 00:44:37.650
maybe taking place in the context of them

00:44:37.650 --> 00:44:38.910
having multiple jobs

00:44:38.910 --> 00:44:41.940
or being under pressure
from property developers,

00:44:41.940 --> 00:44:44.600
finances even in somewhere like London,

00:44:44.600 --> 00:44:47.730
a lot of very very poor
planning decisions are made

00:44:47.730 --> 00:44:50.480
because the mayor of
London is short of cash

00:44:50.480 --> 00:44:52.100
and wants to try to make money

00:44:52.100 --> 00:44:53.950
from property developments illegally.

00:44:55.050 --> 00:44:57.200
Rates money this is rates
money, revenue money,

00:44:57.200 --> 00:45:00.930
and will therefore authorize,
it's huge tower blocks

00:45:00.930 --> 00:45:03.250
and places where nobody
wants to tower block

00:45:03.250 --> 00:45:04.900
and where they can see an
most amount of problems

00:45:04.900 --> 00:45:06.610
but that's the way they make the money

00:45:08.840 --> 00:45:10.840
problems of the poverty of a population.

00:45:10.840 --> 00:45:13.950
So many, many things will get stuck

00:45:13.950 --> 00:45:14.783
because it's very difficult to work out.

00:45:14.783 --> 00:45:16.710
Whether you can generate an income stream

00:45:16.710 --> 00:45:18.420
will people pay for that water rates

00:45:18.420 --> 00:45:19.465
will people pay for their

00:45:19.465 --> 00:45:20.298
(mumbles)

00:45:20.298 --> 00:45:21.720
How do they do that

00:45:21.720 --> 00:45:24.170
problems with different
levels of government?

00:45:24.170 --> 00:45:26.170
So there will often be clashes between

00:45:26.170 --> 00:45:28.720
the sort of sub district
level on the meth team

00:45:28.720 --> 00:45:32.010
and standoffs on who has
legal power to do what

00:45:32.010 --> 00:45:34.670
and finally problems with ideology.

00:45:34.670 --> 00:45:38.410
You will have often somebody
may be very well-meaning.

00:45:38.410 --> 00:45:40.480
They may well not be corrupt.

00:45:40.480 --> 00:45:42.060
They may be trying to do the right thing

00:45:42.060 --> 00:45:46.500
but in Afghanistan, the meth
that I worked with in 2003

00:45:46.500 --> 00:45:48.000
had trained in East Germany

00:45:48.890 --> 00:45:52.390
and his mindset was still
very, very much influenced

00:45:52.390 --> 00:45:54.872
by his training in the 1970s

00:45:54.872 --> 00:45:56.720
and early 1980s in East Germany

00:45:56.720 --> 00:45:59.480
and in particular, his idea
of what it meant to be modern.

00:45:59.480 --> 00:46:00.700
And a lot of what he was doing

00:46:00.700 --> 00:46:05.700
was trying to resist any
attempts for foreigners to impose

00:46:05.770 --> 00:46:07.440
what he thought was an old fashioned

00:46:07.440 --> 00:46:12.120
or sort of, and by modern he meant

00:46:13.160 --> 00:46:16.900
sort of extraordinary
high East German mentality

00:46:16.900 --> 00:46:18.170
of the mid 1980s.

00:46:18.170 --> 00:46:19.760
Anyway, that's just some random thoughts

00:46:19.760 --> 00:46:24.081
but I think locating it in the
culture of the municipality

00:46:24.081 --> 00:46:27.180
and its mindsets is important.

00:46:27.180 --> 00:46:28.310
- I think that's very important.

00:46:28.310 --> 00:46:29.730
And I appreciate that point.

